  "teamId": 120,
  "year": 2023,
  "narrative": "The 2023 Washington Nationals were a team still finding its footing in a rebuilding era, and their 71-91 record reflected that reality honestly. They opened the year stumbling out of the gate with a 10-16 April, leaning on flashes of individual brilliance — most memorably CJ Abrams' grand slam — to keep hope alive in a crowded schedule of competitive games. The offense showed genuine pop, with Lane Thomas, Joey Meneses, and Luis Garcia Jr. providing moments that briefly made Nationals Park feel electric, even as the pitching staff's 5.02 ERA told a harder truth about how most games would ultimately end.\n\nMay was the season's most complicated chapter — a 15-15 wash that masked both promise and pain. Hunter Harvey twice surrendered crushing home runs that erased late leads, becoming an emblem of the bullpen's volatility, while the lineup answered back on multiple occasions with timely long balls from Meneses and Garcia. The team was competitive enough to break even, but the margin for error was razor-thin, and every blown save stung like a season-defining loss in the moment.\n\nJune and July brought the inevitable regression, with the Nationals going a combined 22-30 and the roster's limitations becoming harder to obscure. Yet the darkest month on paper gave way to August's surprising 17-11 burst — the season's brightest stretch — fueled by a rash of clutch hits, a favorable schedule, and contributions from depth players like Alex Call and Jeter Downs who had no business being this fun to watch. For a few weeks, Washington looked like a team ahead of schedule.\n\nSeptember collapsed the illusion, as the Nationals fell 8-18 to close out the year, the youth and the thin pitching catching up with them in the home stretch. The 2023 Nationals were not a playoff team, and never really threatened to be one, but they were occasionally a fascinating one — a squad that could produce a grand slam or a clutch double play one night and surrender a walk-off homer the next. Joey Meneses emerged as a genuine run producer, CJ Abrams showed signs of the player he could become, and the foundation, while still unfinished, grew a little sturdier over 162 games.",
